```

### How does Amber simplify domain models?

Records, sealed types, and pattern matching make expressing domain models concise and safe, so the code written with Loom and Panama is also cleaner.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface PricingResult
    permits Priced, Failed, Pending {}

public record Priced(BigDecimal value, String instrument)
    implements PricingResult {}
public record Failed(String reason)
    implements PricingResult {}
public record Pending(String requestId)
    implements PricingResult {}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;

public class PricingDispatcher {
    public static String handle(PricingResult result) {
        return switch (result) {
            case Priced(var value, var instrument)
                -> instrument + ": " + value;
            case Failed(var reason)
                -> "Failed: " + reason;
            case Pending(var id)
                -> "Pending: " + id;
            // No default: sealed + exhaustive
        };
    }
}
```

### How does Leyden address cloud startup?

Leyden shifts warmup work to build time through CDS and ahead-of-time compilation, so a serverless function starts in milliseconds.

```bash
# Build a CDS archive at build time
java -Xshare:dump -XX:SharedArchiveFile=pricing.jsa \
     -cp pricing.jar com.bank.risk.PricingEngine

# Deploy with the archive for fast startup
java -Xshare:on -XX:SharedArchiveFile=pricing.jsa \
     -cp pricing.jar com.bank.risk.PricingEngine
```

### How do the projects compound in a real system?

Consider a risk-pricing engine at National Bank of India:

| Concern | Project | Benefit |
|---------|---------|--------|
| 100K concurrent requests | Loom | Simple blocking code that scales |
| Flat price arrays | Valhalla | Cache-friendly, no boxing |
| SIMD pricing math | Panama Vector | Multiple lanes per instruction |
| Native analytics library | Panama FFM | Safe, no JNI |
| Domain model | Amber | Records + sealed + patterns |
| Serverless startup | Leyden | Millisecond cold start |

No single project makes this system. The combination makes it simultaneously simple, concurrent, memory-efficient, numerically fast, native-capable, and quick to start.

### Why does understanding the roadmap change how you design?

Knowing Loom is here, you stop reaching for reactive frameworks by default. Knowing Valhalla is coming, you design value-like types anticipating flattening. Knowing Panama exists, you keep numeric work in Java. Seeing the direction lets you build systems that get faster and simpler as the platform advances.

### What should a senior engineer know?

These are coordinated bets, not random features. Understanding which project a feature belongs to tells you its maturity (Loom is final, Valhalla is preview) and its intent. Features ship as previews first and stabilize over releases. Forward-looking design means riding the platform's trajectory rather than accumulating workarounds.
